WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.520
Film Courage: …Here is this lovely bag [hands
Justin the bag with questions in it]

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.850
Justin Warren, Filmmaker of THEN THERE WAS
JOE: Awesome.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.100
I will just pick one at random.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.279
Film Courage: By the way, where can we purchase
this bag?

00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:12.639
Justin: It’s on my website.

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:15.760
Just kidding…it’s my wife’s make-up
bag.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:25.871
[Reads question from bag] This is from Andrey
Savchenko…I’m sorry, I’m sorry, I know

00:00:25.871 --> 00:00:28.850
I’ve butchered your name but I appreciate
your question.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.759
“Hi, what’s the biggest obstacle on your
way in the whole process and how you were

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.520
able to get through it?

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.579
Thank you, Andy?”

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:44.670
Thank you for your question.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.469
What’s my biggest obstacle on my way in
the whole process and how were you able to

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:51.420
get through it?

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.839
Surprisingly I think for me in my feature
film I think the biggest obstacle was really

00:00:57.839 --> 00:01:02.589
just fear.

00:01:02.589 --> 00:01:07.329
That might sound like a cop-out answer but
it’s not.

00:01:07.329 --> 00:01:12.100
I think the hardest part is trusting yourself
throughout the madness of everything that

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.350
is going on.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.110
One of the biggest fears I had was just trying
to come up with the money for it because that

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.049
was a really difficult process.

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:29.060
We did a Kickstarter campaign and we raised
$35,000 for it which I think I had a little

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.720
bit of beginner’s luck because when I look
back at it now I can’t believe that we did

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.000
that, it’s pretty wild.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.850
But I think that the hardest part it’s really
the fear and the patience it takes to sort

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.220
of do one of these things because things are
never going to move as fast as you want them

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.220
to move.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:58.759
One of the things I realized is almost everything
that I thought “Yeah…I should have that

00:01:58.759 --> 00:02:00.100
done in a month.”

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.740
I just started multiplying it by four.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.200
So that should be done in a month and we take
four months.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:18.070
Working at this level (because my film is
a micro-budget film) it’s basically there’s

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:21.470
like that triangle, the good, fast and cheap
triangle.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.160
You can only have two of them.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.540
That is totally a real thing.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.190
And so if you don’t have the money to do
something and you are having someone work

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.920
on it, they’re probably only going to work
on it when they have time to do so.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.870
And that means that you can get it good but
it’s not going to be fast.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:50.040
I noticed that because I think we did really,
really great work on my film and the people

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:55.920
(all the different disciplines) did a great
job but it took a really long time and I think

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.500
that the patience can almost be enough to
drive you mad.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:07.880
For instance our VFX guy who is a brilliant
VFX artist and he works at this big VFX house

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:14.250
was basically working on my film at nights
every other weekend because he had stuff to

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.170
do and he just had a brand-new kid and things
like that.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.730
So that took a long time.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:27.440
My dear friend who is a sound designer did
sound on my film but he was working on that

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.620
throughout all his different jobs so that
was a super slow process.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:39.950
I think just being prepared with patience
(how long it’s going to take) you’d be

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.410
surprised.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:50.330
Waiting for something can drive you mad especially
if that is your number one focus which is

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:55.870
your feature or your project that you’re
working on because it’s only going to burn

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.760
with that level of intensity to you the creator.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.390
It’s not going to burn with that level of
intensity to anybody else.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:09.090
And knowing that and embracing that can allow
you to be cool with it.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:15.280
I think earlier with the post-production process
I didn’t understand that and I would get

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.940
angry and I would get very impatient and I
would get confused like “Why aren’t they

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:21.980
getting back to me?

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:22.980
What’s going on?

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:23.980
Huh?

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.560
I can’t take it!”

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:34.130
Which is just silly because the reality is
anything that is good takes time.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.070
I think the quote is from Alice in Wonderland
but ‘There Are No Shortcuts To Places Worth

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.070
Going.’

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.840
That’s a real thing.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.150
We live in a culture where we want things
and we want it now and we want things instantly.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:53.980
But when it comes to making something like
a feature or just any long-term project it

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:54.980
just takes time.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:00.760
And it takes time for the thing to develop,
it takes time for the thing to marinate and

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.720
the patience and getting through the fear
of it are the two biggest obstacles I’d

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:05.850
say.

